    line: 'A friend thinks you owe them; a former enemy thinks they owe you.',
    gist: 'Friends make dangerous employees and allies, Greene argues, because affection blurs judgement on both sides. A friend you promote may feel entitled rather than grateful, may hide resentment to protect the bond, and knows exactly where you are weak. A former enemy, by contrast, has something to prove: brought onside, they work harder to show they deserve the trust. The law’s logic is that loyalty bought by obligation is fragile, while loyalty earned by a reversal is fierce.',
    scene: {
      who: 'Emperor Michael III and Basil the Macedonian',
      when: '9th century, 856–867',
      text: 'Basil was a peasant from the Byzantine provinces, a strong man and a gifted handler of horses, who came to the young emperor Michael III’s notice and became his closest companion. Michael lifted him from groom to chamberlain, and in 866, after Basil had murdered the emperor’s powerful uncle Bardas, made him co-emperor. Michael had raised his friend higher than any merit would. Basil did not see a gift, he saw a ladder. In September 867 he had Michael killed in his bedchamber and took the throne alone, founding a dynasty that would rule for nearly two centuries.',
    },
    mind: 'Friendship runs on a different mental ledger from business. Between friends we stop counting favours, and to count them feels like a betrayal of the bond. That makes friends easy to give to and hard to hold to account: criticising a friend’s work threatens the friendship, so both sides go quiet. Meanwhile an enemy who changes sides faces a jarring gap between their old opposition and their new role. The mind resolves that gap by committing harder to the new position. Generosity can also turn sour when it is too large to repay, because unpayable debts make people resentful rather than grateful.',
    levers: [
      { name: 'Communal versus exchange norms', text: 'Margaret Clark and Judson Mills showed that in close relationships people are put off by tit-for-tat accounting; we give out of care, not for return. Mix a friendship with a job and those two norms collide. The friend expects the leniency of the first while you need the standards of the second.' },
      { name: 'Cognitive dissonance', text: 'Leon Festinger found that people who act against their earlier beliefs feel tension and ease it by changing the belief. An old enemy who joins you has to explain the switch to themselves. Often the answer they reach is “I must really believe in this”, which makes them more zealous than anyone who was always on board.' },
      { name: 'Benjamin Franklin effect', text: 'Franklin wrote that he won over a hostile fellow legislator by asking to borrow a rare book from him. Having done Franklin a favour, the man seemed to like him more. Bringing a rival into your service works the same way: their own actions persuade them they are on your side.' },
    ],
    traps: [
      { id: 'in-group', how: 'We grade our own people on a curve. A friend’s mistakes are bad luck and their hints of disloyalty are misunderstandings, which is exactly what makes trusted insiders dangerous.' },
      { id: 'halo', how: 'Warmth and shared history bleed into judgements of competence and honesty. You hire the friend because you like them, then mistake liking for evidence.' },
      { id: 'confirmation', how: 'Once you have decided someone is loyal, you file their kindnesses as proof and explain away the warning signs. An enemy gets the opposite treatment, so their real changes of heart go unnoticed.' },
    ],
